Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.43,0:00:05.65,Default,,0,0,0,,Let's turn the metaphor into a test Start with zero and\Napply a Hadamard If we measure
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.66,0:00:10.18,Default,,0,0,0,,now we have equal chances of zero and one We can change\Nthe relative phase and still
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.19,0:00:14.19,Default,,0,0,0,,observe those same probabilities in this basis The\Ndifference becomes visible when we
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.20,0:00:19.43,Default,,0,0,0,,apply another Hadamard before measuring With no phase\Nchange we return to zero With a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.44,0:00:24.03,Default,,0,0,0,,change of one hundred and eighty degrees we obtain one\NWith ninety degrees the outcomes
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.04,0:00:28.78,Default,,0,0,0,,are balanced Do you see the difference Phase can be\Nhidden by one measurement choice and
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.79,0:00:33.19,Default,,0,0,0,,revealed by another An unknown classical coin alone does\Nnot reproduce this behavior